{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790605791,"nonce":"XfAO0rCQqiR4ZwnCP5nFckKyyko=","title":"Hybrid: an interpreted caller passes Ref\u003cf32\u003e to a compiled callee as a pointer to the boxed f64 slot","message":"Haxe compiles an optional basic-typed argument with a default (`x:Single = 0.0`) as an\n`hl.Ref\u003cT\u003e` parameter. When an interpreted function calls a compiled one with such an\nargument, the callee receives the address of the interpreter's 8-byte register slot, which\nholds the value in the interpreter's 64-bit encoding (f32 stored as f64). Compiled code\nlowers Unref/SetRef at the pointee's native width, so a `Ref\u003cf32\u003e` read is a 4-byte load of\nthe low half of a double: round values (0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0) read as 0, others as tiny\ngarbage. `Ref\u003ci32\u003e` happens to work because the int sits in the low 4 bytes.\n\nOnly hybrid mode is affected: `--mode interp` is consistent with itself, and `--mode jit`\nbuilds a real 4-byte slot in the compiled caller.\n\n\n```haxe\nclass S {\n\tpublic var depth:Single;\n\tpublic function new(depth:Single = 0.0) { this.depth = depth; }\n}\nclass Q1 {\n\tstatic function use(s:S):Float return s.depth;\n\tstatic function step():Float {\n\t\tvar acc = 0.0;\n\t\tfor (i in 0...4) acc += use(new S(0.5 * i));\n\t\treturn acc;\n\t}\n\tstatic function main() {\n\t\tvar bad = 0;\n\t\tfor (f in 0...5000) {\n\t\t\tvar v = step();\n\t\t\tif (v != 3.0) { bad++; if (bad \u003c 4) Sys.println('BAD f=$f v=$v'); }\n\t\t}\n\t\tSys.println('bad=' + bad);\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n    haxe -main Q1 -D analyzer-optimize -hl q1.hl\n    ash --mode interp q1.hl                      # bad=0\n    ash --mode jit q1.hl                         # bad=0\n    ash --mode hybrid q1.hl                      # bad\u003e0, count varies with timing\n    ASH_TIERED_ONLY_FINDEXES=\u003cS.new findex\u003e ash --mode hybrid --jit-tier cranelift q1.hl\n                                                 # bad=4517: every call after promotion\n\nDropping the default (`depth:Single`) gives bad=0 in every mode.\n\nThe same shape broke chimpmonk's physics demo in hybrid only (`ShapeDef.new`, `Vec3.new`,\n`Quat.new`, `World.createBody`, `VertexPool.pushVertex` all take `Single` defaults):\nshape depths/radii read as 0 and positions as garbage, giving penetrations around 1.8e5 and\nnon-unit contact normals. `ASH_TIERED_SKIP_FINDEXES` listing those functions restored\ninterp/jit output. `ASH_OSR=0`, `ASH_AIR=0`, `ASH_AIR_NO_WIDEN=1`, `ASH_STUB_COMPILE=1` and\n`ASH_INLINE_ALLOC=0` did not.\n\n\n- Interpreter refs point at the boxed register slot:\n  `crates/ash_interp/src/interpreter/mod.rs` (`slot_ptr` / `from_ptr`, around 6796-6812) and\n  `crates/ash_interp/src/interpreter/ssa.rs` (`CellRef`, around 1387-1395); interpreter\n  Unref/SetRef read/write the full 8-byte box (`ssa.rs` ~1161-1203, `mod.rs` ~6813-6882).\n- `call_compiled_function` (`mod.rs` ~5079-5098) converts only HDYN/HNULL/HDYNOBJ arguments;\n  HREF passes through `value_to_i64` (`natives.rs` ~1209-1234) as the raw slot address.\n- Cranelift Unref/SetRef use the pointee width: `crates/ash/src/cranelift/codegen.rs` ~1926-1936.\n\nLine numbers as of the investigation; locate by function name.\n\n\n- Compiled SetRef of an f32 into an interpreter slot writes 4 bytes over an f64 box\n  (likely source of the very large values).\n- Ref\u003ci64\u003e, Ref\u003cbool\u003e and object refs are probably affected the same way (slot tag bits).\n- Compiled caller -\u003e interpreted callee looks fine: a real pointer arrives and the\n  interpreter reads it at the declared width.\n\n\nAt the interp-\u003ecompiled boundary, give each HREF argument typed scratch storage of the\npointee's real width, call, then write the result back into the register slot. Or make\ninterpreter refs point at typed storage rather than the boxed slot.\n\nA regression case needs an interpreted caller and a compiled callee taking Ref\u003cf32\u003e;\nQ1 with ASH_TIERED_ONLY_FINDEXES=\u003cctor\u003e triggers it deterministically. The hybrid parity\nmatrix is crates/ash_cli/tests/parity_matrix.rs with cases in\ncrates/ash/test/tests/parity_cases.toml.\n\n\n`--mode interp`, `--mode jit`, or `--mode hybrid --jit-tier off`.","files":null}]}